# What the STYX-OVER-THE-RADIO image is: the modules it executes in
# place, and the number of kernel processes it needs.
#
# See image-armv7minit for what these two facts mean and why there is
# one file per init.  os/inferno/init/armv7mstyx.b is the program: one
# board exports its namespace over an AX.25 connected conversation and
# the other mounts it, which is what doc/pycubed-plan.ms calls the whole
# argument.  Which end a board is comes out of /lib/radio by its own
# serial number.
#
# One module: the init loads Radiocal to apply this board's crystal
# correction.  Everything else it uses is a file -- #a, #W, #P and the
# mount -- which is the point.
XIPDIS=\
	lib/radiocal.dis\
	lib/ssl.dis\


# The files this image carries that the root section does not.
#
# ssl.dis, because the conf's root section is carried by EVERY image
# built from it and the shell image has 2240 bytes of flash spare: a
# 1650-byte raw module there would leave 590, which is no margin at
# all.  This is the mechanism os/inferno/port/mkroot grew EXTRAROOT
# for, and the second thing it has bought.
#
# It is in the XIP list above as well, and that is the opposite of the
# call made for mount.dis in image-armv7minit.  Mount is a short-lived
# command run by hand and this image is MEMORY-tight rather than flash-
# tight: it comes to 635744 of 913408, so 277664 bytes of flash spare,
# against a Dis heap holding the init, two relay processes and now an
# ssl connection, in the smaller of two SRAM regions.  XIP'ing ssl.dis
# cost 5864 bytes of that flash and saves its decoded array in RAM.  So ssl.dis costs flash here and not RAM, which
# is the trade this image wants and the reverse of the one that image
# wanted.
EXTRAROOT=\
	/dis/lib/ssl.dis		/dis32/lib/ssl.dis\


# Twelve, after ten was measured too few and fourteen measured too
# expensive -- and the reason it is expensive is the shape of this
# part's memory rather than its quantity.
# The figure matters more here than anywhere else on this board, because
# A PROCESS SLOT IS NOT FREE AND IT IS NOT PAID FOR OUT OF THE HEAP.
#
# os/inferno/port/proc.c does xalloc(conf.nproc*KSTACK) and the pools
# are grown into whatever xalloc has left afterwards.  So fourteen slots
# is 57344 bytes of kernel stack taken before main can have any, and
# that is what starved it: "arena too large: main ... arenasize 79360
# maxsize 81900" and then iallocb unable to find 250 bytes for a
# received packet, with 39516 of the pool's nominal maximum never
# reachable.
#
# Counted, and the count was wrong the first time: the init is one, the
# two relay processes two, poolwatch three, exportfs's exportproc four,
# the three kprocs #a starts seven, and the kernel's own share about
# three -- which is ten, and ten is what I set, and the satellite
# answered a Tversion and then said "no procs for kproc" twice at the
# Tattach.
#
# WHAT WAS MISSED: exportfs starts an exslave per request whenever none
# is waiting (exportfs.c, "if(exq.qwait.head == nil)").  One client with
# one request outstanding settles at one slave, but the first request
# starts it and an overlapping one starts another.  So eleven or twelve.
#
# AND WHY TWELVE AND NOT FOURTEEN.  This part's RAM is two
# non-contiguous regions -- the boot line says so: 65536 at 0x10000000,
# which is the core-coupled memory, and 98304 at 0x20008000 -- and a
# pool's arena cannot span them.  proc.c puts the kernel stacks in the
# second one ("14 kernel stacks of 4096 at 0x20008010"), so every slot
# is 4096 bytes taken out of exactly what the pools have left to grow
# into there.  At fourteen, main reached cursize 42176 against a
# maxsize of 121212 it could never approach, and smalloc sat waiting for
# 300 bytes while the AX.25 link timed out under it.
#
# So the number is not "enough slots" but "enough slots and enough
# SRAM", and this is where the two meet.  The instrument that was
# printing the pools had a slot of its own and now does not: it reads
# #c/memory once, from the init's own process, because an instrument
# that consumes what it measures is worth one reading rather than a
# stream.
#
# #a starts three kprocs when a packet device is attached to it; the
# init is one process; export() serves in the process that called it
# but the mount driver on the other end has its own; and a Styx server
# answering walks and reads concurrently.  The shell image runs two
# processes at five slots, the station seven, and each slot is a
# 4096-byte kernel stack out of about 160K -- so this cannot simply be
# set high.  Neither end runs a shell, which is what makes the number
# affordable at all: at seven a shell AND #a could not coexist.
# ELEVEN, AND THE ONE THING THAT ACTUALLY RETURNS MEMORY.
#
# The pool percentages below are CEILINGS and not reservations -- main
# 118178 plus heap 39925 is more than the arena has -- so lowering them
# frees nothing.  KSTACKPREALLOC takes every process's 4096-byte kernel
# stack from xalloc at boot, so a slot is the only thing here whose
# removal gives memory back to the pools.
#
# Measured in the ssl arm, which is what wants it: the satellite mounts,
# exchanges 229 bytes and then starves, with #c/xalloc reading 6000
# bytes free and exportproc waiting for 406.  One slot is 4096, which is
# ten times the request.
#
# Eleven and not fewer because the count is not guesswork: the init is
# one, the two relay processes two more, exportproc four, #a's three
# kprocs seven, exportfs starts an exslave per request, and the kernel
# keeps about three.  Twelve had five slots of margin over the seven a
# station is known to need; eleven has four.  The failure mode is loud
# -- "no procs for kproc" -- so this is cheap to be wrong about.
NPROC=11

# The pool split.  A satellite runs no shell, so its Dis heap holds only
# the init and two relay processes with 512-byte buffers -- while main
# carries exportfs's buffer per request, two pipe queues, the AX.25
# retransmission window and every Block the radio receives into.
#
# Measured at 50/49/1, which is the shell image's split: main reached
# cursize 42368 with its arena at 79360 of an 81900 maximum, and then
# iallocb could not get the 250 bytes a received packet needs -- "no
# free Blocks / iallocb: no memory 0/40960", with ialloc.bytes zero, so
# the interrupt budget was untouched and it was the pool.  The failure
# lands on the third Styx message.
MAINPCNT=74
HEAPPCNT=25
IMAGEPCNT=1
